Mark Evans writes:
>        Overall, I wonder how we will communicate in 2020.  Will we still
>understnad expression?  Will our children understand the power of
>creativity or will it have gone the way of home births, fresh bread, family
>dinners, and even... Books!

Even if we assume that interface is going to not evolve to incorporate
human facial, vocal and corporal expression (which is an unthinking
assumption, certainly in my book), I still don't think I can ever see
creativity and 'human expression'  vanishing because of the nature of any
future media.  The very success of any medium is, it seems to me, directly
related to how easily these things are communicated via those means.

creativity is by no means being suppressed, even in limited media like
these textual email messages.  I've seen some pretty damn neat stuff
floating around, as I'm sure most people have.  Human creativity is not so
weak as to be smothered or excluded by limitations in communication
methods.  besides, these methods can only improve from their current
status, which already satisfactorily allows creative, inspiring, and
artistic communication.

take a look around!

