From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Sun, 25 Sep 94 20:01:39 WET DST
Subject: [#94-9-495] Cryonics and Metabolic Dependent State Loss

Doug Cutrell writes:
> 
> Simon <levy@cs.utk.edu> writes, in #94-9-490:
> 
> >I have petted live, normally behaving dogs who had
> >been suspended for several hours after complete body washout, at temperatures
> >that (I believe) were too low to support metabolic activity.
> 
> This is very interesting... I was not aware that this had been
> accomplished.  I presume you mean that the body temperature was dropped at
> least 10 degrees C?  It's not clear to me how low the body temperature
> would have to drop before we could be sure that all energy transport
> mechanisms had ceased.  It may be that the temperature would have to
> approach 0 degrees C before we could be sure of this.

   I find this "dynamic refresh" model of memory to be unlikely.
First, human memory is nothing like computer memory. Secondly, it's
not a very fault tolerant mechanism. Why don't people who undergo
electroshock therapy lose all their memory? Finally, there seems to be
many experiments which disprove it (or make it unlikely): the dog
experiments, frozen trained earth worms, people who overdose on
barbituates, cryogenic brain surgery patients, etc.

    I would expect long term memory to be more a hard disk with
slow access, long term storage, but having media errors, and bit-rot.

> In fact, if there seems to be some particular temperature, still above
> freezing, below which the animals cannot be revived, this might be taken to
> be evidence that some sort of metabolic state loss is indeed preventing
> further temperature reductions.

   Or it might be taken as evidence that freezing causes damage to
the cell machinery and prevents revival. If I recall correctly,
cat brains have been frozen for weeks or longer and they still showed
evidence of brain wave activity.
 
> Assuming that this point is satisfied, it would be very interesting to have
> people who have "gotten to know" the dogs judge whether their "personality"
> had been altered by the experience.  And of course, psychological learning
> tests could attempt to ascertain to what degree the dogs' memories had been
> affected by the procedure.

   From what I recall, the dogs were trained to perform tricks such as
jumping through a hoop and they still possessed this skill after
revival. I think trying to look at "personality" is a mistake. First,
it's hard to measure. Second, the animal or person could easily
act different after going through such a trauma. People change personality
after heart attacks and major surgery often because the experience
changed their outlook on life.


